---
import BaseLayout from '~/layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Figure from '~/components/Figure.astro';
import Assumptions from '~/components/Assumptions.astro';
import Callout from '~/components/Callout.astro';
import Math from '~/components/Math.astro';
import Badge from '~/components/Badge.astro';
import Cite from '~/components/Cite.astro';
import SlabDipoleLab from '~/islands/SlabDipoleLab';

const assumptions = [
  { term: 'Structure', value: 'Au(111) slab, 4 layers, a = 4.078 Å, built with ase.build.fcc111; the adsorbed cation layer is drawn for orientation only.' },
  { term: 'Charges', value: 'A planar dipole layer of density p: a sheet −σ in the surface plane and +σ 1.6 Å above it, σ = p/d, each smoothed by a 0.35 Å Gaussian. The metal itself is neutral and not otherwise modelled.' },
  { term: 'Electrostatics', value: 'Planar-averaged Poisson equation d²φ̄/dz² = −ρ̄/ε₀ on a grid of 1200 points per cell; periodic: zero mean field; corrected: zero field in the vacuum. ε₀ = 5.5263 × 10⁻³ e/(V·Å).' },
  { term: 'Signs & units', value: 'p > 0 points from the image charge to the cations (+z). Potentials in V, fields in V/Å, energies in meV per surface cell of the drawn slab.' },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Periodic boundaries and finite size"
  description="Physics Lab P9: a slab with a surface dipole in a periodic cell, the artificial field its images force through the vacuum, how slowly it decays with the vacuum thickness, and what the dipole correction does."
>
  <article class="page-grid lab-page">
    <header class="wide head">
      <p class="eyebrow"><a href="/physics-lab/">Physics Lab</a> · P9</p>
      <h1 class="h-display">Periodic boundaries and finite size</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Surfaces and interfaces are simulated as slabs in a box that repeats in every direction, including the one that
        should be open. When the slab carries a dipole, its copies talk to each other through the vacuum, and the
        numbers depend on how much vacuum you paid for.
      </p>
      <div class="head-badges">
        <Badge label="live-calculation" />
        <Badge label="theory" />
      </div>
    </header>

    <section class="block" id="derivation" aria-labelledby="d-h">
      <h2 id="d-h" class="h-section">Where the artificial field comes from</h2>
      <p>Average the charge over planes parallel to the surface. The potential then obeys a one-dimensional Poisson equation:</p>
      <Math display number="1" tex={String.raw`\frac{\mathrm d^2\bar\varphi}{\mathrm dz^2} = -\frac{\bar\rho(z)}{\varepsilon_0}, \qquad p = \int z\,\bar\rho(z)\,\mathrm dz`} />
      <p>
        If the cell is periodic along <i>z</i>, φ̄ must return to its starting value after one cell, so the field averages
        to zero over the cell. The dipole layer alone would make a potential step of <i>p</i>/ε₀; to undo it, periodicity
        adds a uniform field everywhere:
      </p>
      <Math display number="2" tex={String.raw`E_{\text{vac}} = \frac{p}{\varepsilon_0 L_z}, \qquad \frac{U}{A} = \frac{p^2}{2\varepsilon_0 L_z}`} />
      <p>
        The second expression is the spurious energy per surface area, the slab correction of Yeh and Berkowitz written
        per area <Cite id="yeh1999slab" />. Both fall only as 1/<i>L</i><sub>z</sub>. The dipole correction instead
        demands zero field in the vacuum and places the compensating jump at the cell boundary, inside the vacuum
        <Cite id="bengtsson1999dipole" />.
      </p>
    </section>

    <div class="wide fig-wrap">
      <Figure title="A polar slab in a periodic cell, with and without the dipole correction" labels={['live-calculation', 'theory']} number={1} id="fig-slab" framed={false}>
        <SlabDipoleLab client:visible />
        <Fragment slot="caption">
          Left: the slab and its images above and below; red arrows show the artificial field in each vacuum gap, and the
          green layer is the compensating dipole that replaces it when the correction is on. Right: the planar-averaged
          potential over two cells, and the artificial field against vacuum thickness at the current dipole.
        </Fragment>
        <Assumptions slot="assumptions" items={assumptions} />
        <Fragment slot="source">
          Dipole correction: <Cite id="bengtsson1999dipole" />. Slab Ewald correction: <Cite id="yeh1999slab" />. Structure from ASE; electrostatics solved in your browser.
        </Fragment>
      </Figure>
    </div>

    <section class="block" aria-labelledby="mlip-h">
      <h2 id="mlip-h" class="h-section">Why this matters for MLIPs</h2>
      <p>
        Training data for surfaces and electrochemical interfaces comes from periodic DFT. Whether a dipole correction
        was used, and how much vacuum separated the images, changes the reference energies and forces a model learns.
        A local MLIP cannot see the artificial field directly, but it can absorb its effects into short-range terms that
        then fail to transfer to a different cell size.
      </p>
      <Callout kind="caveat" title="Check the reference settings">
        When a model is trained or evaluated on slabs, the cell length, vacuum thickness and electrostatic boundary
        condition belong in its provenance alongside the functional. The Atlas records them on dataset pages when the
        source states them.
      </Callout>
    </section>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .lab-page {
    padding-block: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 4rem) clamp(3rem, 6vw, 5rem);
  }
  .head .eyebrow a {
    color: inherit;
    text-decoration: none;
  }
  .head h1 {
    margin-block: 0.6rem 1rem;
    font-size: clamp(2.1rem, 4.4vw, 3.6rem);
    line-height: 1.05;
  }
  .lede {
    font: 1.1875rem/1.6 var(--font-serif);
    color: var(--atlas-ink-muted);
    max-width: 44rem;
  }
  .head-badges {
    display: flex;
    gap: 0.35rem;
    margin-top: 1rem;
  }
  .block {
    margin-top: clamp(2rem, 4vw, 3rem);
    font: 1.0625rem/1.7 var(--font-serif);
    color: var(--atlas-ink);
  }
  .block p {
    margin-block: 0 1rem;
  }
  .fig-wrap {
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
  }
</style>
